# Application configuration.
#
# One firmware per unit type on the bench. Exactly one is built at a time,
# selected by that unit's companion conf, which is always applied together
# with its overlay (see README.md, "Building and flashing"):
#
#   TFT unit     boards/nrf5340dk_nrf5340_cpuapp_tft.{overlay,conf}
#   shield unit  boards/nrf5340dk_nrf5340_cpuapp_shield.{overlay,conf}
#
# Both run the same TDMA radio layer (src/tdma) and log soaks to SD.

choice
	prompt "Unit type"
	help
	  Set by the unit's companion conf. CMakeLists.txt stops the build if
	  the choice and the devicetree front end do not match.

config APP_TDMA_CONSOLE
	bool "TFT unit: TDMA field console (ILI9341 + touch + microSD)"
	select COUNTER
	help
	  Field-test console for the TDMA radio layer on the TFT breakout:
	  ILI9341 display, XPT2046 touch and microSD on spi4 / port 0, with the
	  radio shield on spi2 / port 1. Apply
	  boards/nrf5340dk_nrf5340_cpuapp_tft.overlay and
	  boards/nrf5340dk_nrf5340_cpuapp_tft.conf. Soak tests of the continuous
	  TX/RX frame exchange with live link telemetry on the TFT. The unit
	  (MASTER in slot 0, or SEC 1-3) is picked at boot, so one image serves
	  every unit. All PHY parameters are compile-time constants in tdma.h
	  except TX power. Display-only: no UART, logging, or shell.

config APP_TDMA_FIELD
	bool "Shield unit: TDMA field unit (rev 2 shield: OLED + microSD)"
	select COUNTER
	help
	  The same soak runner on the rev 2 shield: an SSD1306 128x32 OLED on
	  i2c1, a microSD card on spi4, and the SX1262 on spi2 / port 1. Apply
	  boards/nrf5340dk_nrf5340_cpuapp_shield.overlay and
	  boards/nrf5340dk_nrf5340_cpuapp_shield.conf. Buttons only, with an
	  essentials-only cycle UI: unit (MASTER in slot 0, or SEC 1-3) at
	  boot, then soak, TX power and an SD summary. UART logging stays on for
	  bring-up.

endchoice

config TDMA_PAYLOAD_LEN
	int "L3 payload bytes per packet (40 or 80)"
	default 40
	range 40 80
	help
	  Bytes of opaque payload behind the 4-byte L2 header. 40 is the
	  engine every log before 2026-10-30 ran. 80 carries four 18 B Opus
	  frames behind the 8-byte clip test header
	  (docs/opus_payload.md): 84 B on air, 12.88 ms of the 18 ms slot
	  window. Time-on-air and the pre-TX pickup follow from it (tdma.h),
	  and so does the soak log record (64 B, or 128 B at 80). Every unit
	  in a group must match: the L2 length check drops anything else.

config SOAK_UNIT_SLOT
	int "Unit to boot as: TX slot 0 (MASTER) to 3, or -1 to pick at power-on"
	default -1
	range -1 3
	depends on APP_TDMA_CONSOLE || APP_TDMA_FIELD
	help
	  Preset the unit for field runs, so it boots without the power-on
	  unit pick: splash (unit and TX power), an SD card check, then
	  straight into the soak menu. 0 is the MASTER, 1-3 are SEC 1-3; give
	  every unit in a kit its own value, e.g.
	  -DCONFIG_SOAK_UNIT_SLOT=0 on one unit. -1 keeps the power-on pick.

config SOAK_BOOT_TX_POWER_DBM
	int "TX power at boot (dBm)"
	default 22
	range -9 22
	depends on APP_TDMA_CONSOLE || APP_TDMA_FIELD
	help
	  TX power each unit starts at; still adjustable from HOME (TX PWR).

config SOAK_TOUCH_CAL
	bool "TFT unit: power-on touch calibration"
	default y
	depends on APP_TDMA_CONSOLE && !SOAK_PAYLOAD_LADDER
	help
	  Run the mandatory touch calibration at power-on. Say n to drive the
	  console from the DK buttons only (B1 UP, B2 DOWN, B3 OK, B4 BACK):
	  calibration is skipped and touch input is ignored, since uncalibrated
	  taps land in the wrong place. Always off in a ladder build, which
	  runs on B1 GO and B4 STOP alone.

choice SOAK_PAYLOAD
	prompt "Soak payload"
	default SOAK_PAYLOAD_RAMP
	help
	  What a soak puts in each transmission's payload. The log's META
	  record says which payload a unit sent. With SOAK_RTT all four
	  sources are linked and the bench port's "mode" command picks the
	  one the next soak starts in; this is the boot default. Without it,
	  only the chosen source is linked. A soak latches its source at
	  start and never switches mid-run.

config SOAK_PAYLOAD_RAMP
	bool "Ramp: payload[i] = base + i"
	help
	  The original payload. The base advances once per staged frame.

config SOAK_PAYLOAD_TONE
	bool "Tone: per-slot PCM tone"
	help
	  Fill each soak transmission with 40 samples of an 8 kHz, 8-bit tone
	  at 330 Hz * (slot + 1) in place of the payload[i] = base + i ramp.
	  Chunk n is the audio for the unit's n-th TX slot of the run, so a
	  missed frame's audio is dropped, never delayed. Rebuild every unit
	  in the run with the same setting, then turn any unit's log into a
	  WAV of its peers with tools/reconstruct_tone.py. See
	  docs/tone_payload_test.md.

config SOAK_PAYLOAD_CLIP
	bool "Clip: a Codec 2 clip uploaded over RTT"
	depends on SOAK_RTT
	help
	  Chunk n carries an 8-byte test header (magic 0xC2, codec, chunk
	  index, clip id) and the clip's bytes [(n mod chunks) * 32, +32):
	  four Codec 2 3200 frames, 80 ms of speech. With 80-byte payloads
	  the chunk is 72 bytes, four 18 B Opus frames (codec 1). The clip
	  is uploaded once per boot with tools/rtt_link.py and loops. A soak
	  will not start in this mode without a valid clip. Score a run with
	  tools/reconstruct_c2.py. See docs/rtt_link_c2_transport.md.

config SOAK_PAYLOAD_LADDER
	bool "Ladder: TX power ladder test, GO/STOP only"
	help
	  The fixed-range TX power test (src/tdma_console/tx_ladder.h).
	  From GO each unit logs its receiver noise floor, then steps its
	  own TX power from -6 to +22 dBm in 4 dB rungs of 30 s, holds
	  +22 dBm for a 20 s tail (4:20 in all) and ends the run itself.
	  Every packet carries its rung, so either unit's log gives RSSI,
	  SNR and loss per rung (tools/ladder_report.py). Both units drop
	  to two buttons: B1 GO, B4 STOP; preset each with SOAK_UNIT_SLOT.
	  Logs are LADR_NNN.BIN.

endchoice

config SOAK_C2_ENCODE
	bool "Encode Codec 2 on the device (encode test)"
	depends on SOAK_RTT
	select FPU
	select FPU_SHARING
	select INIT_STACKS
	select THREAD_STACK_INFO
	select THREAD_RUNTIME_STATS
	select THREAD_RUNTIME_STATS_USE_TIMING_FUNCTIONS
	select TIMING_FUNCTIONS
	help
	  Link the vendored Codec 2 (external/codec2, LGPL-2.1) and add the
	  "pcm" payload mode: the clip buffer holds raw 8 kHz 16-bit PCM,
	  a frame-locked simulated microphone makes each 80 ms chunk
	  available a set time before its TX boundary, and an encoder thread
	  turns it into four Codec 2 3200 frames behind the clip test header.
	  Encode time is logged per TX record. Received chunks are decoded
	  per peer and mixed (c2_dec.c; output discarded until there is a
	  playout path), with per-thread CPU accounting. Test firmware only: the
	  product licensing of Codec 2 is not settled. See
	  docs/c2_encode_test.md.

config SOAK_OPUS_DEC
	bool "Decode received Opus clips on the device (to the HAT earpieces)"
	depends on SOAK_RTT && TDMA_PAYLOAD_LEN = 80
	select INIT_STACKS
	select THREAD_STACK_INFO
	select THREAD_RUNTIME_STATS
	select THREAD_RUNTIME_STATS_USE_TIMING_FUNCTIONS
	select TIMING_FUNCTIONS
	help
	  Link the vendored libopus 1.5.2 (external/opus, BSD-3-Clause,
	  fixed point) and decode every peer's Opus clip chunk on the unit in
	  payload mode CLIP: one persistent decoder per peer, PLC for up to
	  two lost chunks in a row (docs/opus_payload.md, decision 3). With
	  SOAK_AUDIO_HAT the decoded peers are mixed into the earpieces;
	  without, the audio is dropped and only the decode cost is measured
	  (gate C1's decode half). The clips are encoded on the host
	  (tools/c2clip.py opus); there is no device encoder yet. Status
	  reports decode time, PLC counts, stack and state size.

config SOAK_AUDIO_HAT
	bool "WM8960 Audio HAT: mic (payload mode MIC) and earpieces"
	depends on SOAK_C2_ENCODE || SOAK_OPUS_DEC
	select I2C
	select I2S
	help
	  Drive a WM8960 Audio HAT (docs/live_audio_wm8960.md): codec setup
	  over i2c1, i2s0 as the slave at 8 kHz, both mics into a capture
	  ring, and playout of the decoded peers to the earpieces. With
	  SOAK_C2_ENCODE, the "mic" payload mode: the per-frame encoder
	  takes live 20 ms frames (a mono mix, "mix avg|left|right") instead
	  of the PCM clip. With SOAK_OPUS_DEC, the received Opus clips play
	  (no mic mode: there is no Opus encoder on the device yet). Needs
	  boards/audio_hat.overlay after the unit's own overlay.

config SOAK_MIC_DSP
	bool "Mic DSP front end: HPF + SpeexDSP noise suppression + AGC"
	depends on SOAK_C2_ENCODE
	help
	  Condition each 20 ms frame before Codec 2 encodes it, in mic and
	  pcm modes: a 150 Hz high-pass, then the SpeexDSP preprocessor's
	  noise suppression and AGC (external/speexdsp, BSD) on 10 ms
	  sub-frames, adding ~10 ms of delay and ~1.7 ms of CPU per 20 ms
	  frame. On by default once built in; switchable over RTT ("dsp
	  on|off", "dsp hpf|ns|agc on|off"), and off the encoder sees exactly
	  the frames it saw before. Off by default: D1 passed in stationary
	  crowd roar but not in babble (docs/mic_dsp_frontend.md).

# A fourth RTT up channel for the raw mic stream ("rawmic on", rtt_link.c).
configdefault SEGGER_RTT_MAX_NUM_UP_BUFFERS
	default 4 if SOAK_AUDIO_HAT

config SOAK_C2_ARM_FFT
	bool "Codec 2 FFTs through CMSIS-DSP"
	default y
	depends on SOAK_C2_ENCODE
	select CMSIS_DSP
	select CMSIS_DSP_TRANSFORM
	select CMSIS_DSP_BASICMATH
	help
	  Build Codec 2 with upstream's FDV_ARM_MATH path: its FFTs run on
	  CMSIS-DSP (arm_cfft_f32, arm_rfft_fast_f32) instead of kiss_fft.
	  The FFTs were ~39 % of both encode and decode (G0 profile,
	  docs/live_audio_wm8960.md). CMSIS rounds differently from
	  kiss_fft, so frames differ from a kiss-based host encoder in a few
	  more bits. n to go back to kiss_fft for comparison.

config SOAK_C2_PROFILE
	bool "Profile Codec 2 per function (bench command)"
	depends on SOAK_C2_ENCODE
	help
	  Wrap the codec's internal cross-file calls (FFTs, pitch
	  estimation, LSP maths, synthesis) and its libm calls with the
	  linker's --wrap, time each with the cycle counter, and report
	  per-chunk figures after "rtt_link.py bench". The vendored sources
	  are untouched. Adds a little overhead to every wrapped call, so
	  keep it off for timing runs other than the benchmark. See
	  src/tdma_console/c2_prof.c.

config SOAK_CLIP_BUF_KB
	int "Clip buffer size (KB)"
	default 128 if SOAK_C2_ENCODE
	default 64 if TDMA_PAYLOAD_LEN > 40
	default 32
	range 1 256
	depends on SOAK_RTT
	help
	  Static RAM for the uploaded clip. 32 KB holds ~80 s of Codec 2
	  3200; 64 KB holds ~72 s of Opus at 7.2 kbit/s (80 B payloads).
	  The clip must be a multiple of the clip chunk (payload minus the
	  8-byte header: 32 or 72 bytes) and no larger. Raw PCM for the
	  encode test is 16 KB/s, so 128 KB holds 8 s.

config SOAK_RTT
	bool "Soak bench port over J-Link RTT"
	default y
	depends on APP_TDMA_CONSOLE || APP_TDMA_FIELD
	select USE_SEGGER_RTT
	help
	  Stream every soak record to the host over J-Link RTT (up channel 1,
	  the same 64 B records the card gets, META first) and take bench
	  commands on down channel 1: status, soak <minutes> [sd], stop.
	  Replies come back on up channel 2. No pins and no USB stack: the
	  DK's on-board J-Link reads the RAM rings over SWD. A soak started
	  from the UI still logs to the card as well; one started by the
	  soak command logs to RTT only unless given "sd". Drive it with
	  tools/rtt_link.py. Channel 0, logging and the console are left
	  alone. With n, the tree builds and behaves as before. See
	  docs/rtt_link_c2_transport.md.

# RTT channel counts for the bench port: up 0 (Zephyr's terminal, unused),
# up 1 records, up 2 replies; down 0, down 1 commands. Parsed before the
# SEGGER module's definitions, so these defaults win.
config SEGGER_RTT_MAX_NUM_UP_BUFFERS
	int
	default 3 if SOAK_RTT

config SEGGER_RTT_MAX_NUM_DOWN_BUFFERS
	int
	default 2 if SOAK_RTT

# The encode test's libc heap: Codec 2 allocates each state with malloc once
# per run: the encoder, a decoder per peer slot (three) and up to two extra
# decode streams (c2_dec.h). A fixed arena keeps the RAM budget visible (the
# default takes whatever RAM is left); status reports what is left over.
config COMMON_LIBC_MALLOC_ARENA_SIZE
	int
	default 131072 if SOAK_C2_ENCODE

# The encoder (preemptible 0) and decoder (1) threads are preemptible so
# they can never hold off the radio or data threads. They must still outrank
# the UI loop, whose repaints run long, so main steps down to 2.
config MAIN_THREAD_PRIORITY
	int
	default 2 if SOAK_C2_ENCODE || SOAK_OPUS_DEC

# Turning RTT on would otherwise also turn on Zephyr's RTT log backend, a
# blocking writer on channel 0 that stalls every log call while no host is
# attached. The shield unit logs to UART; the bench port leaves that alone.
config LOG_BACKEND_RTT
	bool
	default n if SOAK_RTT

# The TDMA engine keeps the radio awake for its whole life (PLL kept locked
# in FS between slots; the TCXO warm-up cost is paid once at init). The
# driver's sleep-when-idle behaviour would also detach/re-attach its DIO1
# handling at runtime, violating the post-init quiescence L2 relies on
# (docs/tdma_layering.md). This definition is parsed before the driver's
# (sourced via Kconfig.zephyr below), so its default wins.
config LORA_SX126X_NATIVE_SLEEP
	bool
	default n

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
